---
description: Apply when deploying, releasing, publishing a package, or flipping a major feature flag
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- A release is a bet — make it cheap to lose: small increments, watched closely, reversible instantly.
- Before: read the diff since last release as a risk list (migrations, auth, config parsing, serialization → extra care) · everything green on the exact artifact shipping · rollback written down (the command, its duration, and does this change *break* rollback? schema/one-way transforms → expand-migrate-contract) · timing with responders present — not Friday evening, not the peak.
- Stage the exposure, never 0→100: dev → staging → canary → ramp → full, each rung with a pre-defined verdict ("error rate and p95 flat vs baseline for 30 min") — an uncompared canary is just a slow deploy. Feature flags decouple deploy from release: ship dark, flip separately; flag-off is the fastest rollback; ticket flag removal at full ramp.
- After — deploy exit 0 starts the experiment: user-facing signals vs pre-release baseline, the changed component's logs (new error strings are the top alert), the delayed shapes (memory creep, first cron, cache expiry, first peak), and one end-to-end exercise of the shipped feature in prod.
- On failure: roll back first, diagnose second. Roll forward only when the fix is minutes away and understood. A rolled-back release re-ships from the top of the ladder.
- The checklist lives in the repo, versioned, followed by reading it; every incident adds a line. Config changes go through the same ladder — they cause incidents at code's rate. Painful releases mean release smaller and more often, not rarer and bigger.
